#X obj 1800 400 loadbang;
#X obj 1800 450 t b b;
#X msg 1900 500 init;
#X obj 1800 950 spigot 1;
#X msg 1950 900 0;
#X msg 1800 1000 fail c_synth no-lua;
#X obj 1800 1050 s err;
#X obj 2200 400 declare -lib pdlua;
#X text 20 1150 The rules live in c_synth.lua: the choice \, which synth each held key went to \, the velocity switch and the restores. c_synth-lua is its Pd face. Edit the rules there \, not here: test/gate/lua/c_synth_test.lua runs them without Pd. A choice it does not know is fail c_synth bad-synth \, a setting bad-velocity \, a message it does not know bad-selector \, and a Lua error fail c_synth lua-<selector> (C-12)., f 70;
#X text 1200 1150 The choice and the velocity switch are two auto keys \, synth and velocity (ref/module/state.md): the Lua's fourth outlet names the key \, so one list prepend puts either. The Lua takes each restore and answers with the value \, which is put straight back: a load never fills u_state's live store \, so a key nobody re-puts is dropped at the next flush \, and a value that is neither is repaired by the same put. Sections will own both later., f 50;
#X text 1800 1150 At load the Lua answers init with the choice and a bang on its sixth outlet \, which shuts the spigot. No bang means it never loaded -- pdlua missing \, or an error in the Lua -- and fail c_synth no-lua says so on the screen: the nanoKEY then plays nothing., f 40;
#X text 600 1060 t a a: the phone and the pads hear the same choice \, by cords: no bus carries it (C-2)., f 30;
